## 1. Introduction
Antibiotics are recognized as bio-recalcitrant micropollutants and often degraded via advanced oxidation process (AOPs) to avert its terrible environmental risks [1–3]. It is widely accepted that Fenton reaction can provide high-active hydroxyl radicals ( OH)
to mineralize organic molecules, reported as an efficient AOP in treating antibiotics wastewater [4,5]. In terms of Fenton mechanism, Fe(II)/Fe(III) redox cycle is the driving force for the generation of OH from hydrogen peroxide [6–8]. Nonetheless, the sluggish reaction kinetics of $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ reduction (Eqs. 1 and 2) compel the accumulation of Fe(III) sludge and interruption of the reaction [9,10]. Worse still, insufficient free radical ( OH) might bring about the incomplete degradation of polycyclic antibiotics, contributing to potential secondary pollution and ecological threats [11,12]. Hence, the breakthrough in utilizing Fenton reaction into antibiotics elimination should be considered from maintaining $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})}$ regeneration and active species supplement.
$$
\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{H}^{+}\quad\rightarrow\quad\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\cdot\mathrm{OH}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\qquad\mathrm{k}_{1}=40\mathrm{M}^{-1}s^{-1}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Fe^{3+}+H_{2}O_{2}}\quad\rightarrow\quad\mathrm{Fe^{2+}+H O_{2}+H^{+}}\qquad\mathrm{k_{2}=0.02M^{-1}s^{-1}}
$$
In the past two decades, heterogeneous Fenton system has been established to enhance Fenton-type reaction efficiency [13]. Here, Fe ion $\scriptstyle(\equiv\mathrm{Fe})$ is stabilized in iron minerals, Fe-based catalysts or metal organic frameworks (MOFs), suppressing the ion leaching and sludge growth [14]. On the basis of above, tremendous efforts have been submitted to sustain $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ cycle, such as introducing current (electro-Fenton), light (photo-Fenton), electronshuttle catalysts, or chemical reducing agents [15,16]. Considering the cost, operation and hazard, photo-Fenton heterogeneous catalytic system is a potential avenue, in which $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})}$ can regenerate with extra OH production, as described in eqs 3–5 [17].
$$
+\ \mathsf{h}\upnu\rightarrow\mathsf{h}^{+}+\mathsf{e}^{-}
$$
$$
\equiv\mathrm{Fe(III)}+{\bf e}^{-}\ \longrightarrow\equiv\mathrm{Fe(II)}
$$
$$
{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0+{\mathrm{h}}^{+}\rightarrow{\cdot}0{\mathrm{H}}+{\mathrm{H}}^{+}
$$
Apparently, the photo-excited electrons are essential to reduce $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ , but they are prone to recombine with holes or react to active substances in water [18]. Relatively, the electrons are difficult to converge at the iron catalyst surface where Fenton reaction sites are affluent and $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ is formed [19]. Thereby, a large challenge in photo-Fenton catalysts is how to develop a directional and efficient charge transfer pathway. Currently, heterojunction catalysts are proven to induce photo-generated carriers transmission based on the internal electric field [20,21]. Inspired by above assumption, we propose a design strategy that combines oxygendeficient $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ on the surface of MIL-88A MOF to construct sustainable photo-Fenton reaction for antibiotics degradation. Our plan is considered from the following elements: (1) MIL-88A framework is consist of affluent $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})}$ and performs excellent optical/electronic properties, applicable to both Fenton and photocatalytic reaction [22]. (2) MIL-88A can synthesize heterojunction with built-in electron fields through the metal bonds interaction between other compounds [23,24]. (3) $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ is an ideal catalytic assistant due to its strong synergistic effects [25]. The presence of oxygen vacancies can arouse the aliovalent $\mathsf{C e}(\mathbb{U})/\mathsf{C e}(\mathrm{III})$ redox pairs, acting as bridges for the internal electrons transfer [26,27].
Herein, the $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ nanoparticles $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ doping oxygen vacancy) are fabricated under transient ultrahigh temperatures via Joule-heating and thus hybridize on MIL-88A framework through solvothermal methods. Meanwhile, we select oxytetracycline (OC, one typical polycyclic antibiotic) as target pollutant and systematically investigate its removal efficiency in new MIL$88\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(0\mathsf{V})$ heterogeneous photo-Fenton reactions. Significantly, the mechanisms of inner charge transmission, $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})}$ recover and pollutants minimization are revealed via theoretical calculation and experiment analysis.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of $C e O_{2}(O V)$ nanoparticles
The $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ nano-cubes are synthesized followed a reported method. Typically, $\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $(1.96\ \mathrm{g})$ and NaOH ( $_{16.88\mathrm{~g)}}$ were dissolved in ultrapure water $\mathrm{30~mL}$ , resistance $>18\ \mathrm{MW},$ 1 under magnetic stirring for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The solution was then transferred into a $50~\mathrm{\mL}$ high-pressure hydrothermal reactor and reacted at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After cooling, the obtained white precipitate was washed, and dried in vacuum at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then the acquired powder was calcined in a muffle furnace at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to fabricate $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ nanoparticles. The pure $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ cubes were rapidly heated by the radiative heating $(\approx1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\approx1\ s)$ under the joule heating device to induce oxygen defects. Finally, the oxygendeficient ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ samples are respectively named as $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})\mathrm{n}$ $_\mathrm{n}=1,2,3$ , represents radiative heating times).
## 2.2. Fabrication of $M I L{-}88A/C e O_{2}(O V)$ heterojunction
The $\mathrm{MIL{-}}88\mathrm{A/CeO_{2}(O V)}$ composites prepared through a hydrothermal method [23]. Herein, $\ensuremath{1.35\mathrm{~g~}}$ iron chloride hexahydrate $\left(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0\right),$ , $0.58{\mathrm{~g~}}$ fumaric acid $\left(\mathrm{C_{4}H_{4}O_{4}}\right)$ and $0.66\mathrm{~g~}$ modified $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ nanoparticles were mixed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ ultrapure water. Then, the solution was stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ and moved into Teflonlined autoclave, heated at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{~C~}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After centrifugated, washed for 3 times and dried under vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the obtained samples were denoted as MC0,1,2,3, respectively corresponding to pure ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})1$ ,2,3. Additionally, we synthesize purified MIL-88A sample under the same condition without adding ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ .
## 2.3. Photo-Fenton catalytic measurements
In a typical experiment, $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ OC $\mathrm{(30~mg/L)}$ was mixed in a glass beaker $250~\mathrm{mL}$ ). Then, as-prepared catalysts $\mathrm{30~mg},$ were dispersed in above solution. When an adsorption–desorption equilibrium was reached after $20\mathrm{min}$ in dark, a 300 W Xe lamp with an AM 1.5 filter was chosen as a simulated sunlight light source $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ to $800\ \mathrm{nm}_{\cdot}$ and then $50\upmu\mathrm{L}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\left(30\%\right)$ was immediately added. During the catalytic process, $2~\mathrm{mL}$ solution was collected every $20\mathrm{min}$ and then filtrated through a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ water filtration membrane. The OC content was determined at $354~\mathrm{{nm}}$ using the UV spectrophotometer. The single photocatalytic or Fenton reaction was occurred under same condition without involvement of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or light, respectively. Additionally, $10~\mathrm{mM}$ of isopropyl alcohol (IPA), benzoquinone (BQ) and EDTA-NA were respectively added as quenchers of OH, $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ in the free radical trapping experiments. The kinetic rate constants (k) of OC degradation were fitted as following equation.
$$
\ln(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}/\mathsf{C}_{0})=-\mathbf{k}\times\mathbf{t}
$$
where $C_{\mathrm{t}}$ and ${\sf C}_{0}$ are the initial and final OC content of the reaction, respectively. Furthermore, the degradation intermediates were analyzed by a LC-MS/MS (Anglient 1260–6210). After experiment, the catalysts were recycled by centrifugated, washed and drying at $80~\mathrm{^{\circ}C}$ for the cyclic tests and the obtained solution was used for the ion leaching examination by the ICP-MS (PerkinElmer 8300). Additionally, the measurement of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was via N, Ndiethyl-p-phenylenediamine (DPD) sulfate method [28].
## 2.4. Photoelectrochemical testing
UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) were carried on a spectrophotometer (UV-3600, Shimadzu Corporation, Tokyo, Japan). All electrochemical performances were examined on an electrochemical work station (CIMPS, Zahner, Germany), equipped with a three-electrode system: platinum, $\textstyle\mathtt{A g}/\mathtt{A g C l}$ and working electrode. A solution with 0.1 M $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and was chosen as electrolyte to investigate the CV curve, Mott-Schottky plots and the photocurrents response. Then, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was tested in the electrolyte including 0.1 M KCl, $5\ \mathrm{mM}$ ${\sf K}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}$ $(\mathsf{C N})_{6}$ and $5\mathrm{\mM\K_{4}F e\ (C N)_{6}}$ , with working frequency ranging from $100~\mathrm{{mHz}}$ to 1 MHz.
## 2.5. DFT calculation
Density-functional theory (DFT) calculation on catalyst was conducted with the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) with the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) generalized gradient approximation and the projected augmented wave (PAW) method. The kinetic energy cutoff for the plane wave basis was $400\:\mathrm{eV}.$ . Brillouin zone integration on grids with $7\times7\times1$ Monkhorst-Pack k-points was implemented for geometrical optimization and calculation of density of states. The convergence criterion for the electronic selfconsistent iteration and force were set to $10^{-6}$ eV and $0.05\ \mathrm{eV}/\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . The vacuum region $15\textup{\AA}$ in thickness was added perpendicular to surface to avoid the interaction between periodic images. The model of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ and MIL-88A models were obtained from Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre (CCDC). The work functions $(\Phi)$ are calculated with a dipole correction and the heterojunction model is built in a supercell via the software vaspkit_1.3.1 [29]. DFT calculation on oxytetracycline molecular was performed by Gaussian 09 package. Geometry optimization were executed using the hybrid B3LYP method at $6{\-}31(\mathsf{d},\mathsf{p})$ basis set level. Multiwfn_3.8 software was used to analyze the wavefunction of oxytetracycline molecule and visualize the electrostatic potentials and Fukui function [30]. The figure was finally displayed through Visual Molecular Dynamics (VMD) [31].
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structure and characterization
The multi-step synthesis route of the new MIL- ${-88\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(0\mathsf{V})}$ composite is schematically sketched in Fig. 1. The prepared ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ nanoparticle reveals a clear cube morphology with the size of $\sim50~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and multiple well-preserved exposed crystal planes. The oxygen vacancies are present in $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ after heated under $1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ instantaneously. The HRTEM images (inset) observe that the oxygen defects exist in the (111) plane, which can also be verified by the irregular vibrations at (11 1) peak in X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns in Fig. 2a. EPR spectra (Fig. 2b) further affirms that the defects signals strengthen with the instant heating times. Besides, we construct the ideal crystal model of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ according to the vacancy position (Fig. 2c). The calculated partial density of state (Fig. 2d) clarifies that the spin polarization mainly occurs at Ce 4f orbital and it trends to Fermi level in existence of oxygen defects, suggesting that more electrons can be excited and migrate from $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ during the heterojunction forming process [32]. The MIL-88A $/\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(0\mathsf{V})$ composites (denoted as MC) are fabricated via hydrothermal method. As depicted in Fig. 2e and 2f, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ cubes heterogeneously growing on the surface of MIL-88A hexagonal rods with distinct and tight contact interface. Magnified image (Fig. 2g) clears the connection built between (10 0) facet of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ and (110) plane of MIL-88A, indicating the successful formation of heterojunction [32,33]. Additionally, the EDS elemental analysis further confirms that the $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ nanoparticles are uniformly distributed on the MIL-88A framework as presented in Figs. 2h and S1.
Numerous characterizations are conducted to analyze the microstructure of MC in detail. The BET curve in Fig. 3a shows that the MC composite exhibits higher porosity and larger specific surface area than MIL-88A MOF, favorable to the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and antibiotic pollutants in the photo-Fenton reaction. The crystalline structures of the modified MOFs were analyzed by XRD. As exposed in Fig. 3b, the MC composites exhibit dominant peaks at $2\uptheta=10.4^{\circ}$ , $13.0^{\circ},21.0^{\circ}$ , indexed to the (011), (110) and (022) crystal planes of MIL-88A [33]. In addition, other extra peaks can be corresponding to (111), (200), (220) and (331) facets of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ compound, in high coincidence with the simulated pattern of PDF#04-0593 (Fig. S2) [34]. Notably, the MC3 sample displays highest peak signal, demonstrating that the ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ particles with oxygen defect are more beneficial to hybrid on MIL-88A surface. The structure evolution of the as-prepared samples can also be described by the FT-IR spectra in Fig. 3c. Here, the obvious characteristic peaks at 1395, 1605, and $3440~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0,$ $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ functional groups and absorbed water of MIL-88A [35]. The weaker vibration at $793~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represents the stretching vibration of Ce-O metal bond and can be clearly found on the MC composites [36]. Compared with MC0 (loaded with pure $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ ), MC1, 2, 3 samples expose an extraordinary fluctuation in $3240~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ regions. It is probably attributed to the hydrogen bond built between O atom of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ and H atom of MIL-88A around the heterojunction interface [37].
For the sake of clarifying the elements composition and chemical state changes, the related X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted in Fig. 3d-3f. Compared to pristine ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ in Fig. 3d, the binding energies of Ce $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ in $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ are shifted towards low energy by ${\sim}0.9$ eV and the intensity of ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ diffraction peaks at $889~\mathrm{eV}$ and $902~\mathrm{eV}$ are enlarged [38]. It is due to that partial ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ ions are switched to ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ ions to compensate for charge imbalance from oxyanion missing [27,38]. Relatively, the characteristic peaks of O element trends to opposite direction, as displayed in Fig. 3e. In the MC3 composite, the main peaks of Ce and O are red-shifted together, suggesting that charge transfer of Ce is reversible and electrons in $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ are released after coupled with MIL-88A. In addition, Fig. 3f indicates that the peaks of Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ are located at $711.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $726.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ in pure MIL-88A and the satellite peaks around $724.2\ \mathrm{~eV}$ and 718.2 eV represent $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})}$ and Fe(III), respectively [39]. When doping $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ or $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ , two dominant peaks reveal obvious blue shifts and the $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})}$ relative content (the peak area ratio) is increased. All these XPS behaviors in composite can be attributed to the atomic-hybridized electronic interaction at the interface, implying the construction of heterojunction [40,41].
## 3.2. Photo-Fenton catalytic performance
Encouraged by the above characterizations, the photo-Fenton catalytic performance of pure $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ , MIL-88A, and MC heterojunctions are evaluated by the degradation curve of OC in Fig. 4a. Before the photo-Fenton reaction, it is obviously that the adsorption capacity on OC of various samples was distinct, which is mainly linked to the pore volume difference (Fig. S10). Apparently, the MC2 composite is in possession of the highest catalytic efficiency and its degradation rate reaches to $95.76\%$ within $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The corresponding degradation rate constants $(\mathbf{k})$ is up to $0.0508\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ which is 10.81 and 3.13 times as plain $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ and MIL-88A (Fig. 4b). Notably, MC3 sample (with highest oxygen vacancy rate) a slightly weaker degradation property than MC2. It is mainly because that $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ abundant in oxygen defects exhibits stronger adsorption force on $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecular, marginally inhibiting the touch and reaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and MIL-88A [42]. It is also verified by the relationship between O defects content and catalytic performance in Fig. S11. As Fig. 4c unveiled, the catalytic activity of MC2 in photo-Fenton reaction is obviously outstanding than that in only photocatalytic or Fenton-catalytic system, evincing great synergistic effort. Besides, we operating the free radical quenching experiments and the result discloses that OH is the main active species in photo-Fenton reaction for OC degradation while the contribution of $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ is relatively slight. It is in good agreement with the electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) results (Fig. 4d and e), in which MC2 can generate more OH and $.0_{2}^{-}$ than other catalysts under simulated sunlight. The zoomed parts further illustrated that OH provoked from MC2 is simultaneously contributed by Fenton and photo catalytic effect and $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ mostly arises from the latter one. It is in high consistence with the result in $\mathrm{{MC}}2/\mathrm{{H}}_{2^{-}}$ $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{2}|$ dark system (Fig. S12). Moreover, Fig. 4f presents that the degradation efficiency and TOC removal rate of OC are still up to $90\%$ and $75\%$ after 4 cyclic tests while the Fe ion leaching in the solution is still limited less than $0.4~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . Significantly, the excellent performance and stability satisfy our design requirements for antibiotics elimination with high mineralization rate and low iron sludge residue. Moreover, MC2 photo Fenton systems also exhibited high selective removal on other antibiotic pollutants (seen in Fig. S13).
## 3.3. Photoelectrochemical properties
The optimized performance of samples can also be reflected on photoelectrochemical test results. As disclosed in Fig. 5a, the bare MIL-88A shows a good response to visible light and the absorbance around $450\mathrm{nm}$ of MC composites are increased by embedded oxygen defect content in $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ . It is probably explained by that defects in crystal can reform color center and shallow donor state, accelerating higher light energy absorbed and more electrons excited [43]. In terms of Fenton electrochemistry, the CV curve of the catalysts in the electrolyte solution containing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is manifested in Fig. 5b. Clearly, the oxidation peaks of MC2 and MC3 are shifted to low potential and then their haft-wave potential $\left(\operatorname{E}_{1/2}\right)$ is reduced to $0.21\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $0.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ It suggests that the reaction barrier of $\mathrm{Fe(\mathbb{I})}$ and Fe(III) conversion is weakened and the reaction efficiency is greatly promoted, in consistence with the catalytic results [44]. Furthermore, Fig. 5c and d illustrate that the photo-excited carriers are easier to sperate and transfer in MC2 photo-Fenton system, which is verified by higher photocurrent and lower impedance (smaller semicircle). Additionally, we positioned the conduction band (CB) of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ and MIL-88A form Mott-Schottky plots (Fig. 5e and f).
Combining the band gap from Tauc curves (Fig. 6a) and calculated CB, we further obtain the band structure of MC heterojunction (inset and Table 1). It can be seen that the conduction band energy $(\mathrm{E_{C}})$ of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ $\left(-0.16{\:}\mathrm{V}\right)$ is more positive than the standard energy of $.0_{2}/0_{2}^{-}(-0.33\:\mathrm{V})$ . Thus, the $0_{2}^{-}$ generation is thermodynamically inaccessible. Equivalently, OH cannot be engendered in pure MIL-88A in virtue of the valence band energy $(\mathrm{E_{V}})$ of $2.26\mathrm{V}$ $(\cdot0\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{H}_{2}0=2.34\mathrm{\V})$ , corresponding to the previous EPR results. In order to further investigate the built-in electric fields in MC, we calculated the work function $(\Phi)$ of two components according to their exposed crystal planes at interface, as depicted in Fig. 6b and 6c. The result points that the $\Phi$ of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ and MIL-88A is
$6.94~\mathrm{eV}$ and $5.88\ \mathrm{eV}$ respectively, equal to that $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ owns higher Fermi level than MIL-88A. It is worth noting that $\Phi$ of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ is $1.06\mathrm{eV}$ larger than pure ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , suggesting the presence of oxygen defects can encourage $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ forming stronger internal electric field with MIL-88A. As sketched in Fig. 6d and e, the built-in electric field is established via the drawing close of Fermi levels upon contacting and the direction is from MIL-88A towards $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ [45]. On the basis of the above analysis, the charge transfer mechanism in MC photo-Fenton reaction is summarized in Fig. 6f. Herein, the photo-excited electrons accumulated on $\mathtt{E_{C}}$ of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ can migrate to $\mathtt{E_{V}}$ of MIL-88A driven by electric field force. The electron-hole pair separation efficiency is maximized relying on the special Z-scheme electron migration route [40]. Then, massive OH and $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ are generated in the $\mathtt{E_{C}}$ of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ and $\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{B}}$ of MIL-88A, where are respectively suffused with separated holes and electrons. Moreover, the produced $0_{2}^{-}$ can also capture electron and transform to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}=0.69\:\mathrm{V};$ , synergistically promoting the Fenton reaction and OH production. Finally, the targeted antibiotic (OC) is sequentially broken up under the attacking of free radicals.
## 3.4. $F e(\mathbb{T})$ regeneration mechanism
Based on the catalytic stability and charge transfer mechanism of MC2, it is rationally inferred that the photo-excited electrons migrating to MIL-88A might contribute to reducing Fe(III) into Fe $\left(\mathbb{I}\right)$ . To verify this assumption, we firstly construct the theoretical MIL-88A $\mathsf{\Pi}^{\prime}\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ supercell model, as shown in Fig. 7a. After relax optimization, the most stable structure (Fig. S3) unveils that the MIL-88A and $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}(\mathsf{O V})$ molecules are connected via the $c{-}0$ bond $(\sim1.82\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ and partial hydrogen bond, consistent with the FT-IR analysis and XPS results (Fig. S6). The related charge density is illustrated in Fig. 7b. The zoomed image further exhibits Ce atom at interface is in a decreasing electron state, and thus photoexcited electrons are easier to separate from it and migrate to increasing electron region (Fe atom in MIL-88A). As clarified in Fig. 7c, the electron transfer route is $\mathbf{Ce}\rightarrow0\rightarrow\mathbf{C}\rightarrow\mathbf{Fe}$ and the oxygen vacancy close to interface can speed up the electron accumulation. Fig. 7d extrudes the stronger electron snatch ability of Fe than other bordering atoms in MIL-88A, which is properly ascribed to the enhanced hybridization interaction in Fe 2p and 3d orbitals, disclosed by partial density of state in Fig. 7e [46,47]. Thereby, the $\equiv\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})$ in MIL-88A can absorb these electrons (original from Ce atom) to re-transform into $\mathsf{\equiv F e(I I)}$ and the Fe redox cycle with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is sustained.
Our speculation can also be practically supported by the XPS comparison in Fig. 7f and g. It is clear that the shift of Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak in heterojunction after reaction is decreased to $0.38\ \mathrm{\eV}$ from $0.94~\mathrm{eV}$ of MIL-88A and the decreasing of peak intensity is slowed down. It obviously demonstrates that the $\mathsf{\equiv F e(I I)}$ in MC Fentonphoto system can regenerate driven by electrons released from Ce atom, corresponding to the peak variation of Ce $3\mathrm{d}_{5}/_{2}$ Totally, it is worthy to be emphasized that the electron transport in the Fe recycle is occurred between the material interfaces (heterogeneous), minimizing electron loss and iron spillover into solution (homogeneous).
## 3.5. Antibiotics elimination mechanism
Another mechanism discussed in MC photo-Fenton reaction is the mineralization procedure of polycyclic antibiotic (OC). Herein, we engage series of density function theory (DFT) calculations based on optimized OC molecular model, which is described in Fig. 8a and marked by numbers for clear analysis [30]. The calculated electrostatic potential (ESP, Fig. 8b) defined that the positive charge is centered at benzene part (ring Ⅳ) and other regions are negatively charged, suggesting different charged active substances will be adsorbed around the OC surface [48]. From the viewpoint of reaction kinetics, the low HUMO-LOMO gap (0.421 eV) can facilitate electron excitations to orbitals for OC decomposition and the fragmentation of ring Ⅳ is hardest for its highest charge intensity in HUMO state (seen in Fig. 8c) [49]. To further spot the different reaction sites on atoms, we work out three Fukui indexes of OC: $f$ -(electrophilic index), $f^{+}$ (nucleophilic index) and $f^{0}($ (radical attack index), list in Fig. 8d and visualized in Fig. S4. Considering from the free radicals in MC photo-Fenton reaction, the atoms with higher $f^{\mathrm{~-~}},f^{\mathrm{~+~}}$ , or $\textbf{\textit{f}}^{o}$ value are more presumably to be attacked by $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ , $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ , or OH, respectively [50].
Combing the above theoretical speculation and intermediate products detected from HPLC-MS (Fig. S5), we unambiguously speculate feasible degradation pathway of OC in photo-Fenton reaction, as paraded in Fig. 8e. Firstly, the OC decomposition is original from the attack of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ or OH, and the hydroxyl on C13 $(f^{\mathrm{~-~}}=\:0.0247)$ , C17 $(f~{\bar{\mathbf{\alpha}}}=~0.0343)$ or the amide group on $C2$ $(f^{o}=0.0211)$ respectively fall off. After a series of substitution reaction, the intermediate P5 $(m/z=374.10)$ is obtained, as well as the by-product P4 $(m/z\ =\ 61.20)$ . Then, the following reactions are focused on the ring-open of OC and occurred from the ring Ⅰ to ring III (the low-charge zone in HUMO state). In this process, OH consecutively occupies and cleaves C18, C17, C16 and C8 reactive sites (with relatively high $\boldsymbol{f}^{\mathrm{~}0}$ value) to simplify polycyclic structure, forming the compound P7 $(m/z\:=\:260.10)$ , P11 $(m/z\ =\ 172.09)$ , P12 $(m/z=146.11)$ and P14 $(m/z=108.06)$ respectively. Furthermore, $0_{2}^{-}$ and OH simultaneously invade the C9 $(f^{\mathrm{~+~}}=0.0810$ , $f^{o}=0.0466)$ , achieving the cracking of benzene ring. The singlestranded product P16 $(m/z=112.13)$ is then broken into smaller molecular and ultimately mineralized into ${C O_{2}}/{\mathrm{H}_{2}O}$ , minimizing the organic residues and secondary pollutions.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ nanoparticles with rich oxygen defects are synthesized under transient heating and grow in situ on the MIL88A organic framework to form the MIL-88A $/C{\sf e O}_{2}(0\mathsf{V})$ heterojunction. The prepared composites characterize well-preserved crystal structure and optimized photoelectric properties. The $\mathsf{C e}(\mathbb{T})/\mathsf{C e}$ (III) redox pairs and built-in electron field at the interface can accelerate carrier transmission between atoms and accomplish the heterogeneous reduction of Fe(III), exhibiting significantly enhanced catalytic activity and recycle property. Thus, oxytetracycline is efficiently degraded with kinetic constant of $0.0508\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and remains high mineralization rate after 4 cyclic tests, driven by the coupling effect of photocatalytic and Fenton reaction. This work provides novel insights into designing new self-repairing photo-Fenton catalysts for polycyclic antibiotics elimination with high efficiency and stability.
